# Untitled

Metal Oxide-Coated Three-Dimensional Graphene Prepared by the Use of Metal–Organic Frameworks as Precursors**
Owing to its unique structure and properties, graphene, a two-dimensional (2D) \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\)-hybrized carbon sheet, has been widely investigated.[1] Moreover, composites of graphene with many nanomaterials have been successfully prepared by various methods. Such composites exhibit improved properties and/or enhanced performance as compared to each individual component in the composite,[2] since the graphene sheet acts not only as a template or substrate with a large surface area for anchoring nanomaterials, but also as an electrically conductive channel for fast electron transfer.[3] Recently, three-dimensional (3D) graphene-based composites have attracted increasing interest, since they are not only electrically conductive, but also macroscopically porous, thus allowing ready access by gas molecules or electrolyte ions.[4] For example, 3D graphene networks (3DGNs)[5] synthesized by chemical vapor deposition (CVD) have exhibited high thermal conductivity[6] and good electrical and mechanical properties.[5a,7] Furthermore, the obtained 3DGNs can also be
used as templates for the growth of various nanomaterials, such as oxides, sulfides, hydroxides, and carbon nanotubes.[8] However, the morphologies of reported metal oxide/3DGN composites normally showed that the 3DGNs were coated with thick films of metal oxide particles, rods, or flakes. To the best of our knowledge, until now there has been no report on the preparation of porous metal oxide nanostructures coated on 3DGNs without agglomeration.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) that exhibit unique characteristics have been widely studied.[9] Composites of MOF and graphene oxide (GO), referred to as MOF/GO, in which the GO serves as a matrix for the well-dispersed growth of nanosized MOF crystals,[10d] have been synthesized.[10] Furthermore, when the GO was reduced, the reduced graphene oxide (rGO) obtained overcame the poor electrical conductivity of the MOF; thus, MOF/rGO composites can be used as promising electrode materials for electrocatalysis.[11] Herein, we report a simple method for the synthesis of metal oxides on 3DGNs. The application of the resulting materials, i.e. metal oxide/3DGN composites, in photocatalysis and lithium-ion batteries (LIBs), was also explored.
By using our simple method, a 3DGN (see Figure S1 in the Supporting Information),[5b] a porous 3D graphene structure, was used as a template for the preparation of the MOF/3DGN composite (Figure 1a, step 1). After annealing of the MOF/3DGN composite first under argon and then in air (Figure 1a, steps 2 and 3), the metal oxide/3DGN composite was obtained. As a proof of concept, the well-known MOFs ZIF-8[12] and MIL-88-Fe[13] were used for the preparation of ZnO/3DGN and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composites, respectively. As a result of the unique properties of 3DGNs,[5] the metal oxide/3DGN composites were electrically conductive and also possessed good mechanical properties (Figure 1b–e).
Figure 2a shows the morphology of the 3DGN after the growth of ZIF-8: ZIF-8/3DGN exhibited a rougher surface as compared to that of the original 3DGN (see Figure S1b). The crystal structure of ZIF-8 was confirmed by X-ray diffraction (XRD; see Figure S2).[12,14] Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) also indicated the presence of elemental zinc (see Figure S3). ZIF-8 nanocrystals with a polygonal shape and a size of approximately \(500\;\mathrm{nm}\) were coated on the surface of the 3DGN (Figure 2b). By changing the synthetic conditions, the size of the ZIF-8 nanocrystals could be tuned from about 100 to about \(900\;\mathrm{nm}\) (see Figure S4).
After annealing of the ZIF-8/3DGN composite under argon gas, the surface of the obtained sample was more textured and rougher (Figure 2c). Nanoparticles (NPs) with a size of approximately \(10\;\mathrm{nm}\) were generated (Figure 2d). Selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern indicated
that these NPs have an amorphous structure (bottom inset of Figure 2d). Raman spectra showed that the intensity of the D-band increased after annealing of the ZIF-8/3DGN composite in argon (see Figure S5), thus suggesting that the organic component of ZIF-8 was converted into amorphous carbon coated on the surface of the NPs. The amorphous carbon could serve as a buffer that impedes the further contraction of ZIF-8 nanocrystals during annealing.[15]
Figure 2e,f shows the porous metal oxide/3DGN composite obtained after subsequent heat treatment of the sample in Figure 2c in air. The obtained porous metal oxide structures were a bit smaller than the ZIF-8 nanocrystals obtained after annealing in argon (Figure 2c). This difference in size can be attributed to the decomposition of amorphous carbon in the sample in Figure 2c during annealing in air and the contraction of the metal oxide structure during the cooling process.
The XRD pattern, high-resolution TEM (HRTEM), and the SAED pattern further confirmed that the porous metal-oxide structure coated on the 3DGN was composed of \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) NPs. The XRD pattern (see Figure S6) showed the distinct (100), (002), and (101) diffraction peaks of \( \mathrm{ZnO} \), thus indicating that the hexagonal phase of \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) (JCPDS 79-0205) was formed.[16] The HRTEM image of a small \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) particle (top inset of Figure 2 f) shows a lattice spacing of approximately \( 0.25\;\mathrm{nm} \), which matches the (101) plane of \( \mathrm{ZnO} \). Four clear diffraction rings were observed in the SAED pattern (bottom inset of Figure 2 f) and can be assigned to the (100), (101), (110), and (103) planes of \( \mathrm{ZnO} \), respectively.
Thus, a novel \( \mathrm{ZnO/3DGN} \) composite was obtained by using the MOF ZIF-8 as the precursor of \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) and a 3DGN as the 3D template. Moreover, since the size of the ZIF-8 nanocrystals growing on 3DGNs can be tuned by changing the
synthetic conditions (see Figure S4a,c,e), the size of the porous \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) structures in \( \mathrm{ZnO/3DGN} \) composites can also be tuned (see Figure S4b,d,f). Although a ZIF-8 with a small size of approximately \( 100\;\mathrm{nm} \) could be synthesized on a 3DGN (see Figure S4g), only \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) NPs rather than porous \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) structures were obtained after the two-step annealing process (see the inset in Figure S4h).
As another sample, a \( \mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN} \) composite was also obtained after two-step annealing of MIL-88-Fe/3DGN (Figure 1a; see details in the Experimental Section). MIL-88-Fe was successfully coated on the surface of a 3DGN to form a MIL-88-Fe/3DGN composite (Figure 3a). The crystal structure of MIL-88-Fe was confirmed by XRD (see Figure S7a). In the obtained \( \mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN} \) composite (Figure 3b), the presence of \( \alpha\mathrm{-Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) was confirmed on the basis of the SAED pattern, HRTEM, and XRD (Figure 3c,d; see also Figure S7b). The SAED pattern shows four clear diffraction rings, which can be assigned to the (012), (104), (110), and (113) planes of \( \alpha\mathrm{-Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) (Figure 3c, inset). A lattice spacing of approximately \( 0.25\;\mathrm{nm} \) corresponding to the (110)
plane of \(\alpha\mathrm{-Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) was observed in the HRTEM image (Figure 3d, inset).[17]
\(\mathrm{ZnO}\) is an important photocatalyst owing to its low cost, nontoxic nature, and high photochemical activity.[18] In this study, the photocatalytic properties of the \(\mathrm{ZnO}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite were examined through the photodegradation of the dye methylene blue (MB) under irradiation with UV light. For comparison, the pure 3DGN and \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) , which was obtained by the two-step annealing of ZIF-8 (see Figure S8), were used in control experiments.
Figure 4a shows the normalized change in the concentration of MB \((C/C_{0})\) during the photodegradation process; \(C\) and \(C_{0}\) are the measured concentration of MB after photodegradation for a certain time and the original concentration of MB, respectively. It is evident that the \(Z\mathrm{nO}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite exhibited better photodegradation performance than did the pure 3DGN and \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) . After irradiation of the dye solution with the 3DGN, \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) , or \(Z\mathrm{nO}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) for \(60\,\mathrm{min}\) , almost all of the dye was decomposed by \(Z\mathrm{nO}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) , whereas only about 12 and \(80\,\%\) of the dye was decomposed by the 3DGN and \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) , respectively (Figure 4a). The improved performance could be attributed to the high surface area of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite and prevention of the recombination of photoinduced holes and electrons owing to the good contact and effective interaction between the 3DGN and \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) (see Figure S9).
Recycling of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite was quite convenient as a result of the good mechanical properties and robust structure of the 3DGN (Figure 1). After the photodegradation process, the \(Z\mathrm{nO}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite could be picked up easily from the dye solution with tweezers, then washed thoroughly in Milli-Q water (Figure 4b) and dried for
the subsequent photodegradation experiments. The durability of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite was tested (see Figure S10). After four consecutive cycles, there was no obvious change in its photodegradation performance, thus indicating the good stability of the photocatalytic properties of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite.
\(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) is a promising anode material for lithium-ion batteries (LIBs).[19] The combination of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) with graphene has been proved to further improve the lithium-storage properties, with higher specific capacities and better cycling stability.[20] In this study, the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite was tested as the anode for LIBs, in which the 3DGN served as the current collector. The \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) powder used in the control experiment was synthesized by two-step annealing of MIL-88- Fe (see Figure S11) and then pasted on Cu foil with carbon black and a binder for LIB-anode testing. Figure 5a shows the first three discharge/charge curves for the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite at a current density of \(0.2\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) between 0.01 and \(3\,\mathrm{V}\) (versus \(\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}\) ). In the first discharge curve, the two sloping voltage plateaus at approximately 1.5 and \(1.0\,\mathrm{V}\) can be ascribed to the structural transformation of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and the insertion of lithium ions, respectively.[19a] The voltage plateau at approximately \(0.8\,\mathrm{V}\) corresponds to the conversion of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) into Fe. This reaction is reversed in the subsequent charging process, as reflected by a sloping plateau at approximately \(2.0\,\mathrm{V}\) in the first charge curve.[20a] These results are in good agreement with previous studies.[20,21] A small charge/discharge plateau found at about \(0.1\,\mathrm{V}\) resulted from lithium intercalation/deintercalation between graphene layers.[22]
The cycling behavior of the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) electrodes is compared in Figure 5b. After 50 discharge/ charge cycles at the current density of \(0.2\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\)
electrode showed a fast decay of the capacity to \(261\;\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}}\) in the 50th cycle, whereas a reversible capacity as high as \(864\,\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}}\) was retained for the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite
(approximately \(76\,\%\) of the initial capacity of \(1133\,\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}}\) ), thus indicating good cyclability of the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite.
The rate capacities of the electrodes were also measured. The \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite electrode exhibited 10th-cycle discharge capacities of 871, 854, 785, 718, and \(587\,\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}}\) at current densities of 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, and \(5\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively (Figure 5c), thus implying a good rate performance. The capacity of the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite at high current densities, such as 2 and \(5\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , was comparable with and even higher than those of composites of iron oxides and graphene.[20b,21,23] In contrast, the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) electrode provided a worse rate capacity (Figure 5d).
The areal density of 3DGNs (ca. \(0.65\;\mathrm{mg\,cm}^{-2}\) ) is much lower than that of the commonly used copper current collector (ca. \(10\,\mathrm{mg\,cm}^{-2}\) ).[24] This feature makes 3DGNs promising for the development of lightweight LIBs, as they enable the total weight of the electrode to be reduced and also provide high capacity per unit mass of electrode. For example, at a current density of \(0.2\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite provided a capacity per gram of the electrode of \(864\,\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}}\) after 50 cycles, whereas only \(24.3\,\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}}\) was observed for the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) electrode (as based on the total mass of the current collector, including active materials, a conductive additive, and a polymer binder).
In summary, we have developed a simple method to prepare novel metal oxide/3DGN composites by the use of MOFs as the precursor of the metal oxide and 3DGNs as the backbone through a facile two-step annealing process. As a proof of concept, the obtained ZnO/3DGN and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\) 3DGN composites were used in photocatalysis and a lithium-ion battery (LIB). We believe this method could be used for the synthesis of other metal oxide/3DGN composites with 3D structures simply by the appropriate choice of a specific MOF as the precursor.
## Experimental Section
Chemicals: Zinc nitrate \((\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2})\) , 2-methylimidazole (MIM, \(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{N}_{2})\) , ferric chloride \(\mathrm{(FeCl}_{3})\) , terephthalic acid (H2BDC, \(\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4})\) , methanol, and \(N,N\!\cdot\) -dimethylformamide (DMF) were purchased from Sigma–Aldrich. All of the reagents were used without further purification.
Synthesis of ZIF-8 crystals: \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) \(\left(100\,\mathrm{mmol\,L}^{-1}\right.\) , \(15\,\mathrm{mL}\) ) and MIM \(\left(100\,\mathrm{mmol\,L}^{-1}\right.\) , \(30\,\mathrm{mL}\) ) were stirred in methanol at room temperature for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) . The resulting ZIF-8 crystals were collected by centrifugation, then washed with methanol several times and dried in a vacuum oven at room temperature.
Synthesis of MIL-88-Fe crystals: A mixture of \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}\) (243 mg) and H2BDC (166 mg) in DMF (10 mL) was heated at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in an oil bath for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and then allowed to cool naturally to room temperature. The resulting MIL-88-Fe crystals were collected by centrifugation, then washed with ethanol several times and dried in a vacuum oven at room temperature.
Synthesis of 3D graphene networks (3DGNs) by CVD: The preparation of 3DGNs was based on our previous work.[5b] Briefly, the nickel foam was annealed at \(1000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) in a CVD chamber, which was filled with a mixture of the gases Ar (200 sccm) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) (40 sccm). The furnace was then allowed to cool down naturally to \(950\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . When the temperature of the furnace was stable, ethanol was bubbled into the furnace tube with a flow of Ar (10 sccm) for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) . The furnace was then cooled down fast to room temperature at
a cooling rate of about \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\), and 3DGNs coated on Ni foam were obtained. For the preparation of a 3DGN electrode for LIBs, the graphene growth time was elongated to \(60\,\mathrm{min}\), and the furnace was allowed to cool down naturally to room temperature. To remove the Ni foam, the synthesized sample was first dipped into a solution of poly(methyl methacrylate) (PMMA; \(4.5\,\mathrm{wt\,\%}\) PMMA with a molecular weight of approximately 996000 in anisole) for several seconds. After the anisole had evaporated, the PMMA-coated sample was immersed in the etchant solution (mixture of \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}\) \((1\;\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) and HCl \((2\,\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) in water) at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to remove the Ni foam. Finally, the PMMA coated on the 3DGN was removed by hot acetone vapor and subsequent annealing at \(450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under Ar (200 sccm) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) (40 sccm).
Synthesis of ZIF-8/3DGN composites: Prior to the synthesis of MOF/3DGN composites, the 3DGN was first heated in \(69\,\mathrm{wt\,\%}\) \(\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\) at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) overnight. The acid-treated 3DGN was then placed in a glass bottle, and menthol \((1\,\mathrm{mL})\) was added, followed by methanolic solutions of \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) \((100\,\mathrm{mmol\,L}^{-1}\) \(0.5\,\mathrm{mL})\) and MIM \((100\,\mathrm{mmol\,L}^{-1}\), \(1\,\mathrm{mL})\). After \(12\,\mathrm{h}\), the ZIF-8/3DGN composite was picked up with tweezers, rinsed with methanol thoroughly, and dried in a vacuum oven at room temperature. The growth temperature and the concentration of the \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) and MIM were tuned for the synthesis of ZIF-8/3DGN composites with various sizes of ZIF-8 coated on the 3DGN.
Synthesis of MIL-88-Fe/3DGN composites: \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}\) \((243\,\mathrm{mg})\) and H2BDC \((166\,\mathrm{mg})\) were first mixed in DMF \((10\,\mathrm{mL})\). Acid-treated 3DGN was immersed in the mixture, which was then heated at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in an oil bath for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\). The solution was allowed to cool down naturally to room temperature, and then the MIL-88-Fe/3DGN composite was picked up with tweezers, rinsed with ethanol, and dried in a vacuum oven at room temperature.
Synthesis of metal oxide/3DGN composites: The synthesized MOF/3DGN composite was placed in the center of a quartz tube and annealed under Ar protection with a gas flow rate of \(250\,\mathrm{sccm}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The annealed MOF/3DGN composite was subsequently heated in air for \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(380\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to give \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{O})_{3}\mathrm{DGN}\) or \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}\).
Photocatalytic degradation of a dye: A suspension of \(\mathrm{ZnO}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite \((1.5\,\mathrm{mg})\) and methylene blue \((15\,\mathrm{mL},\,15\,\mathrm{mg\,L}^{-1})\) was first stirred in the dark for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) and then irradiated with a full-spectrum xenon/mercury–xenon lamp (Hamamatsu Photonics K.K.). The concentration of methylene blue was calculated according to Beer's law on the basis of the absorption peak at \(664\,\mathrm{nm}\) for methylene blue by using a UV-1800 UV/Vis spectrophotometer (Shimadzu).
Lithium-ion-battery measurements: A solution of \(\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}\) \((1\,\mathrm{mol\,L}^{-1})\) in ethylene carbonate/diethyl carbonate (1:1 in volume) was used as the electrolyte, and pure lithium foil was used as both the counter and the reference electrode in two-electrode coin cells. The galvanostatic discharge/charge measurements in the voltage window of \(0.01{-}3\,\mathrm{V}\) were carried out with a Neware battery tester (BTS-5V5mA, Neware, China). The \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}\) composite was used directly as the working electrode, without the use of additional conductive materials or binders. In the control experiment, the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) powder obtained by two-step annealing of MIL-88-Fe was used as the working electrode. To obtain the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) electrode, a slurry prepared by mixing the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) powder with poly(vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF) binder and conductive carbon black (8:1:1, weight ratio) was coated on Cu foil and dried in a vacuum oven.
Characterization: The morphologies of the samples were characterized by SEM (Model JSM-7600F, JEOL) and TEM (JSM-2010, JEOL). Raman spectra were collected with a WITec CRM200 Raman System (488 nm laser, \(2.54\,\mathrm{eV}\), WITec). XRD tests were performed on an XRD-600 X-ray diffractometer (Shimadzu).
Keywords: composite materials \(\cdot\) lithium-ion batteries \(\cdot\) metal–organic frameworks \(\cdot\) photocatalysis \(\cdot\) three-dimensional graphene networks
